preConditionHook:
// 'Strong closes mean swing it, weak closes mean scalp it', recast as a
// binary scalp-versus-hold exit: if the ENTRY BAR did not close as a strong
// with-trend trend bar, take the scalp -- get out on the entry bar itself or
// within maxBars bars of it, once the trade is showing a profit.
//
// LABELLED HONESTLY: Brooks's actual point is about position SIZE (swing part
// of it, scalp the rest), which the engine cannot express. This is the binary
// approximation of that, not a translation of it.
//
// THE BAR TESTED IS THE ENTRY BAR, at offset BarsSinceEntry. That variable
// lag is the whole machinery of the rule: at BarsSinceEntry = 0 it is the
// current bar, at 1 the bar before, so the same bar's strength governs both
// chances to exit.
//
// eb IS CLAMPED TO maxBars BEFORE IT INDEXES ANYTHING. EasyLanguage does not
// short-circuit, so 'bse <= maxBars and weak(close[bse])' would still read
// close[bse] at whatever offset an old open position has reached, and past
// Max Bars Back that raises in both libraries. The clamp keeps the read
// inside a small fixed window while the UNCLAMPED bse still gates the
// condition, so the behaviour is unchanged and the read is always legal.
//
// ctx.BarsSinceEntry() is 0 WHILE FLAT and never a -1 sentinel (measured over
// 13,630 flat bars), which is why the MarketPosition guard carries the flat
// case rather than the counter.
//
// DIRECTION-ADJUSTED, refining the merged 'sides: same': a long is weak when
// the entry bar failed to close as a strong BULL bar, a short when it failed
// to close as a strong BEAR bar, so the two sides carry different text and
// each guards its own MarketPosition -- the shape ExitOnWeakFollowThrough
// uses. Body fractions are products, not quotients: EL raises a runtime
// divide-by-zero and a zero-range bar is legal data.
//
// Not WeakEntryBarScratchExit, which fires only at BarsSinceEntry = 0, judges
// the body by its ABSOLUTE size (so a strong bar against the position passes
// it) and has no profit term. Not NumBarsIfProfitable either: elapsed bars
// plus a profit sign, with no bar-strength test at all.
bse = ctx.BarsSinceEntry();
eb = bse;
if (eb > (int)maxBars) eb = (int)maxBars;
weakBull = el_le(close[eb], open[eb])
    || el_lt(close[eb] - open[eb], strongBodyFraction * (high[eb] - low[eb]));
weakBear = el_ge(close[eb], open[eb])
    || el_lt(open[eb] - close[eb], strongBodyFraction * (high[eb] - low[eb]));
